\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Dr Janine Anstey\'92s coffee had cooled to the point she could sip it while she waited for an R&D archive website to load. She thought nothing of the delay, and turned her attention to some paper-based file notes. She was halfway through her coffee when she checked the screen again; the spinner was still spinning.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 No matter. It was that time in the afternoon when she\'92d call home and settle dinner plans. She already had a conversation in her head, negotiating her way out of Tex Mex again, when she was startled by the abrupt electronic tone of connection failure.\
A lab assistant appeared in her doorway. \'93Comms are down,\'94 she said. \'93Still got internal systems though.\'94\
The afternoon sailed off course by tolerable degrees. A scheduled delivery failed to arrive; comms to \'93upstairs\'94 was still down. At six, the lobby by the surface lift began filling with people trying to go home. R&D people are by nature a patient bunch, and no one would risk expressing a fear that the lift had failed without evidence to back it up. But, before long, nervous attempts to avoid eye contact had become nervous attempts to get anything to make sense on their tablets.\
Everyone went back to their desks or to kitchens. Beer o\'92clock was declared, and the low tides in old whiskey bottles had been assessed and lowered again.\
By ten, no one had yet expressed what was on their minds. Some went quietly to bunks they kept for overnighters; when those were full, others fell asleep in couches, office chairs or under their desks.\
By the following morning, they hypothesised that the lift shaft had been physically sealed. They forced the surface lift\'92s doors open; they could not get it to move. They opened the emergency hatch in the roof of the lift car; they saw and smelled the concrete.\
There had been an accident on the surface, they hypothesised; something about the foyer remodelling; it would not take long for rescuers to dig them out.\
Food was collected from kitchens, offices and emergency stores. Medicines were counted. Lights and power still worked, and taps were at full pressure. Staff took hot showers and soaked.\
Hermann and Anstey assembled everyone in the cramped conference room and gave them a full briefing about TPlus, how it improved tissue repair, metabolic performance, organ function and resistance to physiological stress.  They explained it was their hope for giving the surface more time, if needed, to correct whatever issues they were having up there. It could not replace food, but they felt it could be efficacious in reducing the caloric intake required for survival and could therefore extend the rations they did have. They reminded everyone that TPlus has recently passed \cf0 \outl0\strokewidth0 \strokec2 its initial human safety trials
\f2 \outl0\strokewidth0 , so taking it would not be mandated. Hermann and Anstey would be taking the drug.\

\f1 \cf2 Everyone opted in.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 For the first few days Hermann treated the situation as an unexpected trial. He took blood, recorded weight, measured organ function and made quiet predictions shared only with Anstey or anyone who posed direct questions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 People lost weight, but minor injuries healed. A technician\'92s chronic shoulder pain disappeared. A skin condition cleared up without the usual medication. Infections that should have become troublesome did not.\
Hermann discovered he could read without his glasses and, without glasses, he amended his models.\
Food stocks diminished, but daily consumption fell below four megajoules without deterioration.\
They stopped using parts of the laboratory. Lights went out in rooms that no longer served a purpose. People slept where they worked. Emergency rations replaced ordinary meals.\
They continued taking TPlus. Hermann and Anstey administered the doses.\
People became thinner without looking properly sick. Skin remained intact. Old damage repaired. Hermann\'92s grey hair began turning dark. Janine lost a scar on her knee and the stiffness in two fingers.\
Others died.\
Hermann recorded these things.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Anstey also had other, quieter work. The more that TPlus stock reduced, the greater her need to protect the data. If they were going to perish here, there were certain matters about TPlus that she\'92d rather resolve, and leave no untidy trails for others to follow without the proper context. She would not be around to provide the proper context, and that was as much as she allowed herself to dwell on about it.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 First she needed the TPlus batch records gone, and they were gone. She removed all linked trial records, then TPlus production data. She removed the production architecture, payload records, synthesis information, and enough of the supporting work that a later researcher could not reconstruct the programme from what remained. When she had finished, TPlus had ceased to exist as a recoverable programme.\
Anstey went after holes in the archive, and the orphaned references. Anyone sufficiently interested might determine that something had been removed, but they would not be able to rebuild it.\
The mood of  the laboratory was dark by then. Hermann and Janine occupied only a small part of it. Both had lost considerable weight. Neither looked as ill as they should have.\
Hermann no longer wore glasses, and his hair was better than when they were sealed in. He continued recording his results. Anstey let him.\
There was very little food left. Nothing remained for either of them to do about that.
\f0\fs24 <!$Scr_Ps::1>}